\newlabel{sec:transfer}{{9}{12}{Transfer: a negative result}{section.9}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {11}{\ignorespaces The positive control our detection-agreement instrument had never been given. ``Same-box'' is the fraction of frames on which two objects' prompt chains select the identical box; a high rate means the grounding stage carries no object identity. Run against \emph  {every} counterpart rather than only the ones that fire, the instrument separates cleanly --- and the split is interpretable: the chains that collide are the cans and bottles, the ones that separate are the boxes. Deployed binding is blind \emph  {within} a shape class, which is the regime LIBERO-Object's groceries occupy. Small $n$ (only $4$--$6$ of $60$ frames have both chains detecting) makes these demonstrations of discrimination, not calibrated rates.}}{12}{table.caption.47}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:poscontrol}{{11}{12}{The positive control our detection-agreement instrument had never been given. ``Same-box'' is the fraction of frames on which two objects' prompt chains select the identical box; a high rate means the grounding stage carries no object identity. Run against \emph {every} counterpart rather than only the ones that fire, the instrument separates cleanly --- and the split is interpretable: the chains that collide are the cans and bottles, the ones that separate are the boxes. Deployed binding is blind \emph {within} a shape class, which is the regime LIBERO-Object's groceries occupy. Small $n$ (only $4$--$6$ of $60$ frames have both chains detecting) makes these demonstrations of discrimination, not calibrated rates}{table.caption.47}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {7}{\ignorespaces Layer 4 drawn rather than argued. The released head asked for three objects emits three place points, $14.5$ and $13.0$\,cm from the basket. The basket itself is \emph  {not} fixed --- it is re-placed every episode, with a per-task diameter of $3.66$\,cm (max $3.96$) --- so a memorised place point is wrong by $14$\,cm against a target that only ever moves by $4$, and $14$\,cm is enough to release a grocery over open table. Radial distances are measured; angles are for legibility.}}{13}{figure.caption.40}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:placepoint}{{7}{13}{Layer 4 drawn rather than argued. The released head asked for three objects emits three place points, $14.5$ and $13.0$\,cm from the basket. The basket itself is \emph {not} fixed --- it is re-placed every episode, with a per-task diameter of $3.66$\,cm (max $3.96$) --- so a memorised place point is wrong by $14$\,cm against a target that only ever moves by $4$, and $14$\,cm is enough to release a grocery over open table. Radial distances are measured; angles are for legibility}{figure.caption.40}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {12}{\ignorespaces Changing only the OpenGL backend MuJoCo renders through --- same seed, same trials, same weights, same package versions. The aggregate is identical and $40\%$ of the individual episodes are not. Matching a published rate is therefore weak evidence of having reproduced an experiment, and any paired statistic computed across a renderer change is invalid, because pairing assumes the trials are the same trials.}}{13}{table.caption.48}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:renderer}{{12}{13}{Changing only the OpenGL backend MuJoCo renders through --- same seed, same trials, same weights, same package versions. The aggregate is identical and $40\%$ of the individual episodes are not. Matching a published rate is therefore weak evidence of having reproduced an experiment, and any paired statistic computed across a renderer change is invalid, because pairing assumes the trials are the same trials}{table.caption.48}{}}
